(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609092100-a-refusal-that-named-the-wrong-instruction")
  (:adr/title "A literal pool on every native target, and the refusal that named the wrong instruction")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-09")
  (:adr/decision "The four rodata literal heads are admitted on every `:native` execution
target on both ISAs, in amu's `rodata-literal-targets`, in
kotoba-verifier's target scope, and in kotoba-mir's target selection.
AArch64 reaches the pool with `adr`, one instruction, with an explicit
out-of-range refusal rather than a truncating wrap.

`kernel-function-address` is SPLIT out and its target set is unchanged,
because kotoba-mir still refuses it on AArch64. Closing that is a
separate act with its own measurement.

Landed: kotoba-mir #63 (4a049cc9), kotoba-native #168 (c14a49f3) and #169
(ADR 0046 amendment), kotoba-verifier 6f4871f3, amu (pins + gate split).")
  (:adr/related "kotoba-native ADR 0046 (amended); kotoba-mir ADR 0016, ADR 0018; kotoba-kir ADR 0233 (the accumulation tree as contract)")
  (:adr/body "A refusal that says \"the layout pass does not model X\" is a statement about
an implementation on the day it was written. This one said it for four
weeks and was wrong about which instruction X even was.

## What was refused

Four heads -- `ucs2`, `guid`, `bytes-literal`, `bytes-literal-length` --
put bytes the program carries into a pool the toolchain places, and answer
its address. They were admitted on the two aiueos x86-64 packagers and
nowhere else, by THREE separate gates that all said versions of the same
thing:

  amu   `rodata-literal-targets`     -- an image-resolved address requires
                                        a native aiueos x86-64 target
  verifier  the aiueos-only head set -- bounded kernel memory operation
                                        requires the aiueos kernel target
  mir   `:rodata-address-target-mismatch` -- AArch64's answer is ADRP+ADD,
                                        whose 4 KiB page split the layout
                                        pass does not model

The third named its own blocker, and named it so that closing it would be
a deliberate act rather than something that quietly never happened. That
was the right thing to write. It was also wrong.

## The blocker was the wrong instruction

`adr Xd, label` reaches +/-1 MiB in ONE instruction with no page
arithmetic at all. ADRP+ADD exists because a linker cannot know that a
symbol is near, so it buys +/-4 GiB by splitting the address at a 4 KiB
page boundary.

This backend knows what a linker does not. `resolve-program-layout`
places the literal pool at the end of the SAME emitted buffer as the
code, so the distance from any instruction to any entry is bounded by the
size of one emitted program. A megabyte of Kotoba is enormous, and if a
program ever exceeded it, `a64-adr` refuses with
`:rodata-literal-out-of-range` rather than truncating -- a wrapped 21-bit
field produces a VALID instruction that reaches the wrong address, which
is worse than either refusing or crashing.

Four bytes rather than seven, and the displacement is measured from the
address of the instruction itself rather than from its end.

## And the hosted targets already had a pool

The first gate's premise was that a pool needs an image with no dynamic
loader. A pool needs a CONTIGUOUS MAPPING that holds the code, and a
hosted target has one: the kexe loader mmaps the code buffer and jumps
into it. Its own context carries `code_base` and `code_length`.

`:native` execution turns out to cover twelve targets -- linux, macos,
windows, android and ios on both ISAs, plus the two bare `kotoba-v1`s --
and `compile-source` was measured on all twelve rather than argued about.
Only two of the twelve are VERIFIABLE (`kotoba.verifier`'s
`target-contracts` holds `:x86_64-kotoba-v1` and `:aarch64-kotoba-v1`),
but that was already true for every operation and is a different
question.

## A pool that is addressable and unreadable is not a pool

The verifier had a second refusal waiting behind the first: a region's
base must be granted by the caller, not chosen by the program. A literal
is neither -- and the distinction is the whole of why it is admitted.
`4096` is an address the program picked and could have picked
differently; `(bytes-literal \"...\")` is a relocation the backend resolves
into a pool it placed beside the code, which the program cannot make
point anywhere else, and whose bytes are its own.

## The one gate that did NOT move

`kernel-function-address` shared the literals' gate from the day it
arrived, correctly: both need a backend that resolves a label. It stopped
being correct here, because `kotoba.mir` still refuses
`:gmir/function-address` on AArch64 under its own keyword. Widening one
gate for both would have admitted at `amu check` what `amu compile`
refuses. The operation set and the target set were therefore SPLIT, each
with its own sentence, and the subset relation is asserted rather than
assumed.

## What it was for

kotoba-native's `elf64` docstring says of IQ3_XXS, IQ3_S, IQ4_XS and
IQ2_S -- 306 of the shipping model's 866 tensors -- that they \"decode
through codebook grids that `qwen35_quant_tables.inc` holds as static
const data, and this dialect has no rodata and no bytes literal to put
one in. Those types stay in the C.\"

They no longer have to. Measured 2026-09-09 on this host (Apple Silicon,
AArch64), through the SIGNED path -- `amu compile` -> `sign` -> `run`
with a measured runtime and a trust file:

  `kvalue` over `kvalues_iq4nl`, read out of the program's own pool:
    0 -> -127,  5 -> -35,  8 -> 1,  15 -> 113          (table exact)

  `nibble-sum` over 128 bytes = one IQ4_XS block's 256 packed nibbles,
  granted as a region by the caller:
    kotoba -663, independent reference -663

  control 1: one nibble changed 14 -> 15
    kotoba -639, and -639 - -663 = 24 = kvalues[15] - kvalues[14]
    (so the codebook is INDEXED, not summed as a constant)

  control 2: the same bytes truncated to 64
    kotoba -210, reference -210
    (so the granted LENGTH bounds the walk)

The same source compiles for x86-64 as well; it cannot be executed on
this host.

## The rule this is an instance of

CLAUDE.md already carries it: before using a rule as a constraint, decide
whether it states a property or an implementation state, and if it is an
implementation state, MEASURE IT BEFORE OBEYING IT. Three gates here were
implementation states. Two of them named their reason and one of those
named the wrong instruction -- which is the failure mode that is hardest
to see, because a refusal with a stated reason reads as a decision.")))
